"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has released a public preview of 'Mistral Large 4', a next-generation open-weight multimodal model with 1 trillion parameters specialized for coding, agent workflows, cybersecurity, and more. Mistral Large 4 (ML4) is a native multimodal open-weight model utilizing 52 billion active parameters out of 1 trillion total parameters. Trained from scratch using 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs across its own data centers in Europe, its model weights are scheduled for official release later this month. Optimized for enterprise environments including cybersecurity, finance, and legal sectors, and trained on data in more than 160 languages. Introducing Timestral Mistral has unveiled 'Timestral', a family of time-series foundation models that support past and future covariates and deliver outstanding zero-shot forecasting performance. With a maximum size of 209M parameters, it ranked first across major public benchmarks (GIFT-Eval, TIME, fev-bench), surpassing existing SOTA models. Available in four sizes—5M, 28M, 123M, and 209M—it can run efficiently even on CPUs and offers remarkable accuracy relative to model size. It combines synthetic data pre-training, covariate-aware attention, and long-context training, with support for fine-tuning via Mistral Forge. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral opens a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest industrial economy—to accelerate collaborations in Physics AI and industrial AI technologies. Mistral established a specialized hub for Physics AI and industrial AI in Munich to foster collaboration with Germany's key industries, including automotive, energy, and manufacturing. To secure Europe's AI sovereignty, the company plans to build 1 gigawatt (GW) of European computing infrastructure by 2030 and provide open-weight models that can run on customers' own infrastructure. Following the acquisition of Emmi AI in May 2026, research personnel were integrated, and collaborations are currently underway with BMW in crash simulations and Siemens Energy in industrial AI applications. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla have formed a partnership to introduce Mistral AI models into 'Smart Window', Firefox's AI browsing assistant. Mistral models are integrated into Mozilla Firefox's AI browsing assistant 'Smart Window (beta)' to support organizing complex searches and tab-based information exploration. It will first be provided to users in France and North America, with plans to expand to the United Kingdom and Germany later this year. It provides multilingual models optimized for regional languages, dialects, and cultural contexts, with privacy protections applied by default, such as conversations not being saved and zero data retention. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral and Cloudera have formed a partnership to enable the deployment and training of specialized AI models while ensuring the sovereignty and security of enterprise data. Enables inference execution across on-premises, public/private cloud, and air-gapped environments by integrating Mistral's models into Cloudera's hybrid data platform Supports the secure utilization of decades of proprietary customer data to build customized models while maintaining ownership of the models and intelligence Meets the demand for Sovereign AI by keeping data, compute, models, and operations under customer control Websites are closing their doors to AI agents While Big Tech companies are rushing to release autonomous AI agents, major websites are sequentially blocking their access and operations citing security and revenue model protection. Companies like OpenAI, Meta, Google, and xAI are introducing AI agents capable of web browsing and executing tasks, but they are facing blocks from major web services. Yelp only permits paid data licensing programs, while airlines and financial institutions such as Delta Air Lines, JPMorgan Chase, and Charles Schwab are also restricting agent access for security reasons. Amazon is engaged in legal battles while blocking access by services like Perplexity, Meta's Muse, and xAI's Grok, citing website policy violations and decreased ad impressions. DeepSeek Just Released V4.1-Flash-1017, Scores 77.0 on DeepSWE, Opens Search API DeepSeek released its latest checkpoint model, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017, significantly improving performance and cost efficiency, and opened Search and Fetch APIs to developers. DeepSeek launched DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017 across web, app, and API, featuring a 1M token context, improved benchmark scores, and faster task completion speed. Scored 77.0 on DeepSWE v1.1, surpassing Claude Opus 5, with gains of 2.0 to 11.1 points across 11 reported benchmarks compared to the previous September release. Opened Search and Fetch APIs to all developers, providing an affordable coding model and web layer together under a single API key. 20 Jev Alternatives: The Decision Models Taking On TypeSafe in 2026 A comparative analysis guide covering 20 alternative decision models that emerged following the release of TypeSafe's Jev model, which outputs only decisions such as labels and scores rather than generating text. After TypeSafe released Jev on September 15, 2026—a decision model that performs parallel evaluations without text generation—more than 20 alternative models were introduced within a month by companies such as Cloudflare, Liquid AI, and OpenAI. Jev has limitations including no image support, no self-hosting capability, undisclosed model size, and no fine-tuning support, which alternative models are addressing. Cloudflare Clef demonstrates competitiveness in specialized tasks, outperforming Jev in narrow classification benchmarks (BANKING77 F1 score of 94.20 vs. Jev's 79.74), though Jev still holds the advantage in complex reasoning (GPQA Diamond). StepFun Releases Step 5 Preview Open Weights: 600B MoE, 27B Active, 1M Context StepFun has released Step 5 Preview, an open-weight MoE model for agentic tasks featuring 600B total parameters, 27B active parameters, and a 1M context window. StepFun released the open weights (FP8, BF16) of Step 5 Preview on Hugging Face and ModelScope, specialized for long-horizon agentic tasks, software engineering, and financial workflows. Featuring a MoE architecture with only 27B active parameters per token out of 600B total parameters, it supports up to a 1M-token context and requires datacenter-grade GPUs (at least 4x B300 or 8x H200 for FP8) for self-hosting. While demonstrating competitive coding performance on par with open-weight rivals GLM-5.3 and Kimi K3, and showing strengths in long-context reasoning (AA-LCR), it showed weaknesses in terminal benchmarks and specific domains such as legal and SRE. Perplexity Open-Sources SPACE, a Firecracker MicroVM Sandbox for AI Agents Perplexity has open-sourced SPACE, an in-house developed Firecracker MicroVM-based sandbox platform designed for the secure isolation and code execution of its AI agents. SPACE (Sandboxed Platform for Agentic Code Execution) is a platform released on GitHub under the Apache 2.0 license that provides a completely isolated environment from the host by allocating an independent Firecracker MicroVM for each agent task. It is designed to prevent sensitive information from being exposed within the guest environment by controlling credential (secret) injection and outbound traffic at the host-side gateway and network layer. Through btrfs CoW-based snapshots and warm pool management, median creation latency in production was significantly reduced from 185 ms to 60 ms (and from 447 ms to 89 ms at p90). It proved strong security isolation performance by recording zero successful escapes out of 108 VM-to-host escape attempts across nine frontier models. DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 - An MoE Model Introducing CED Architecture and Native Image Understanding An article regarding the DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 MoE model, which incorporates the CED architecture and native image understanding capabilities. Announcement of the DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 model Application of the CED architecture Support for a Mixture of Experts (MoE) structure capable of native image understanding Fitting a Book into an 88x31 Button with ImageMagick An introduction to using ImageMagick to fit book images into 88x31 retro web buttons Covers techniques for creating small 88x31 web button images using the ImageMagick tool Unable to verify detailed body content due to an access verification prompt Is it worth staying after the main conference if I don't have workshop registration? (NeurIPS) [D] A post seeking advice from a researcher who only holds a NeurIPS main conference ticket without workshop registration on whether it is worth staying on-site after the main event concludes. Due to tickets selling out even before the NeurIPS paper review results were released, the author was unable to register for workshops despite being an accepted main-track paper author. The author inquires whether there are useful networking opportunities available if one stays on-site with only main conference registration and no workshop registration. The author is contemplating whether it is better to cut the trip short and leave immediately after the main conference ends if there is no significant practical benefit. EpochGo: Local-first multi-agent AutoML engine using offline Ollama LLMs and deterministic Scikit-Learn pipelines [P] Introduction to EpochGo, an open-source, local-first multi-agent AutoML engine that combines offline Ollama LLMs with Scikit-Learn pipelines to resolve data privacy concerns and hallucination issues. Designed to separate the reasoning layer (qwen2.5-coder:7b powered by Ollama) from the execution layer (Scikit-Learn Pipeline), ensuring the LLM analyzes only schema metadata rather than directly accessing raw data. Generated Python code undergoes AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) verification prior to execution to prevent arbitrary system command execution and leaks outside the sandbox. Automatically generates ready-to-infer FastAPI endpoints following optimized model pipeline evaluations, running 100% offline on standard hardware environments with 16GB RAM. How customer contract lengths and GPU useful life affect AI infrastructure financing Analyzes the structural risks that discrepancies between GPU useful life, customer contract durations, and debt maturities pose to AI infrastructure financing and securitization. AI infrastructure financing heavily depends on whether GPUs can sustain rental revenues long enough to recover depreciation and debt financing costs. While CoreWeave's August $2.6 billion loan maturity is approximately five years, underlying customer contracts average only three years, leaving lenders to bear renewal risk. Moves to securitize and create liquidity around AI infrastructure exposures could expand capital raising, but risk triggering vulnerabilities reminiscent of the 2008 financial crisis. Anthropic bets voluntary scrutiny sets AI oversight terms before regulators act Anthropic is pursuing a strategy to take the lead in setting AI oversight standards through voluntary scrutiny and verification ahead of action by regulatory authorities. Anthropic aims to lead voluntary review and oversight frameworks before government regulations are introduced. The goal is to secure leadership in shaping future AI regulatory standards through preemptive verification measures. Information in the provided post body is limited. OpenAI’s London staff receive average of £2.3m per person annually This news reports that OpenAI London branch employees receive an average of £2.3 million per person annually. A post was shared on the Reddit community stating that the average annual compensation of OpenAI London office employees reaches approximately £2.3m per person. It demonstrates the extraordinary level of compensation offered by major AI tech companies to attract and retain core AI talent. People who refuse to use AI will fall behind An article presenting the view that those who refuse to adopt AI will eventually fall behind due to a decline in productivity and competitiveness. Stubbornly refusing to use AI incurs a significant opportunity cost in terms of growth opportunities, time savings, productivity, and competitiveness. A phenomenon of 'creative destruction' similar to how those who failed to adapt during the early adoption eras of computers and email were left behind is recurring. Those who succeed the most will be the ones who combine human expertise and judgement with AI, rather than blindly relying solely on automation. From a single artificial neuron to Transformers: The wild evolutionary tree of AI, and what comes next ? Reflecting on the evolutionary process of AI architectures from a single artificial neuron to Transformers, this piece examines the next paradigm leap that will follow Transformers. Major obstacles have triggered paradigm shifts in AI, from perceptrons and the XOR problem to LSTMs overcoming the vanishing gradient issue, up to attention-mechanism-based Transformers. Looking beyond Transformers—currently at the apex of the AI ecosystem—it raises questions about what form the next-generation architecture replacing self-attention might take and the possibility of its emergence. Sharing a timeline resource detailing the history of development from perceptrons to text and video generation models, it invites community discussion on the post-Transformer era. Why BioWare's Neverwinter Nights still has thousands of active players An article exploring why BioWare's classic RPG Neverwinter Nights maintains an active community and thousands of active players more than 20 years after its release. Neverwinter Nights is a commercial hit and critically acclaimed title from BioWare, the studio behind Baldur's Gate, Mass Effect, and more. While it is mentioned less frequently among gamers compared to the studio's other major titles, it still maintains an exceptionally tight-knit and active community more than 20 years later. The body of the article cuts off at 'Read full article', so only the introductory section discussing the secrets behind its longevity is included. This Hidden Device Could Free Up Gigawatts of Grid Power An article explaining the importance of adopting Variable Frequency Drives (VFDs), which control motor speed to reduce power waste and meet soaring electricity demand from data centers and other sectors. Approximately 50 million electric motors, accounting for nearly one-third of total U.S. electricity demand, are unnecessarily running at full capacity at all times, wasting massive amounts of power. Installing Variable Frequency Drives (VFDs) that automatically adjust motor rotation speed based on demand can reduce motor energy consumption by about 40 to 60 percent. Due to retrofitting burdens and other factors, only 16% of industrial facilities and 4% of the commercial sector have adopted VFDs; however, with securing grid capacity becoming urgent due to the expansion of data centers and electric vehicles, the need for regulations and incentives is being emphasized. Reverse Engineer Anything Project Brings AI Agents to Binary Analysis The open-source project REA has been unveiled, enabling AI coding agents to integrate with reverse engineering tools such as Ghidra, Hopper, and IDA to analyze compiled binaries. The open-source project 'Reverse Engineer Anything (REA)' utilizes MCP servers to allow AI agents like Claude Code or Codex to interact with disassemblers and analyze binaries. It runs in a local environment and supports static analysis not only for Ghidra, Hopper, and IDA, but also for JavaScript/Electron applications and .NET assemblies. Within the developer community, reactions are mixed: while some welcome the enhanced performance compared to existing agents, others express concerns over easier unauthorized cloning and cracking of commercial apps, as well as forecasts that big tech will accelerate transitions to the cloud. Amazon Acquires DuckLabs and Integrates DuckDB in Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL Amazon acquires DuckLabs, the creator of open-source analytical database DuckDB, and pursues integration with Aurora PostgreSQL and cloud services. AWS acquired Amsterdam-based DuckLabs, and the founders will continue to steer the open-source technical direction after joining AWS. DuckDB and the related Duck Stack (DuckLake, Quack, etc.) will remain free and open source under the MIT license, continuing under the oversight of the non-profit DuckDB Foundation. DuckDB is optimized for ultra-fast queries on data of 1 terabyte or less, and is expected to play a critical role in agentic AI workloads that involve frequent data exploration. [New AI Models] 5 New Releases Including BFL's 'FLUX.1 Tools / Flux 3 Image Klein' Five new AI models have been unveiled, including Black Forest Labs' Flux 3 Image Klein, Step 5 Preview, and Ling 3.1 Flash. Black Forest Labs released the weights for 'Flux 3 Image Klein', an image editing model with 5.6 billion parameters capable of running on a single consumer GPU and supporting 4K generation. Task- and development-specialized models featuring MoE architectures with 1 million token context windows released their weights, including StepFun's 'Step 5 Preview' (600 billion parameters) and Ant Group's 'Ling 3.1 Flash' (560 billion parameters). Krea introduced the beta version of 'Krea 3', capable of 2K image editing in under 8 seconds, while BytePlus unveiled 'OmniHuman 2.0', which generates videos of up to 5 minutes reflecting vocal emotion and rhythm. LLMs Alone Aren't Enough for AI Coding: Replit Validates Code with 'Deterministic Simulators' AI coding companies such as Replit are adopting deterministic simulator-based verification platforms to overcome the probabilistic limitations of LLMs and ensure the reliability of generated software. To verify the proper functioning of AI-generated software, Replit is leveraging the 'deterministic simulation testing' platform from verification startup Antithesis. By artificially inducing issues like network failures in a proprietary hypervisor-based virtual environment and precisely reproducing the conditions that trigger errors, it enhances the reliability of code fixes and validation. The trend of combining deterministic control and verification mechanisms—such as predefined rules or simulations—rather than relying solely on the probabilistic judgment of LLMs is spreading across AI coding and enterprise agents as a whole. GPT-6 Astra Solves 98% of Unreleased Physics Problems, Even Identifying Expert Errors OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra autonomously solved 49 out of 50 highly challenging, unreleased theoretical physics problems, even correcting an error in a human expert's reference solution. An international joint research team including ICTS and Google DeepMind evaluated the autonomous problem-solving capabilities of major AI models using 'TheoPhysEval', a benchmark spanning 11 subfields of theoretical physics. GPT-6 Astra achieved a 98% (49/50) accuracy rate in an environment with web search and code execution; during error review, its final score was upgraded after pointing out a calculation error in the expert reference answer. While proving that AI can autonomously verify and solve complex numerical calculations and equations from academic papers, limitations were still noted in higher-level research design capabilities, such as selecting promising research topics or redirecting research directions. Former OpenAI Researcher Claims “AI Internals Can Be Read”… Sparking Both Hope and Skepticism A former OpenAI researcher has asserted that AI internals can be interpreted, arguing that neurons in AI neural networks are not tangled in disorder but rather that humans simply failed to recognize their shared underlying principles, drawing mixed reactions of anticipation and skepticism. Nick Cammarata, a former OpenAI researcher, argued in an essay that neurons previously thought to bundle unrelated concepts actually possess an organized structure that detects shared features (e.g., sharp V-shapes). Preliminary analysis of a language model suggested that approximately 90% of analyzed neurons perform a single primary function, and experiments were also conducted using AI agents to predict and steer neuronal activation. Reactions to this claim remain mixed: while some view it with high anticipation as a pivotal turning point in AI alignment research, others counter that it has limitations such as missing information when it comes to fully understanding the entire model. OpenAI Enhances 'ChatGPT' Extension for Chrome... \"Analyzes Open Tabs and Controls Directly\" OpenAI has upgraded its ChatGPT extension for Google Chrome, unveiling features that enable analyzing open tabs and directly controlling browser actions. It can simultaneously analyze content across multiple open tabs in Chrome and perform Q&A tasks, such as generating comparison tables. In addition to 'Chat' mode, the 'Work' mode handles actual browser control tasks including filling out forms, sending messages, downloading files, and organizing tabs. Users can connect Chrome via 'Settings → Computer Use' in the ChatGPT desktop app and install the official extension to use it as a sidebar. Old Smartphone in the Drawer Turns into a 'Bipedal Robot'... \"For Just 500,000 KRW\" UCLA researchers have developed 'PhoneBot', an open-source compact bipedal robot that utilizes an Android smartphone as its brain and sensors, lowering production costs to around 500,000 KRW. By utilizing the smartphone's camera, IMU sensor, and processor to replace expensive vision computers and sensors, robot manufacturing costs were reduced to approximately 400 USD (about 530,000 KRW). Equipped with a total of 13 low-cost servo motors (6 on each leg, 1 for torso rotation), it achieved stable walking learning through reinforcement learning simulations. It works properly even with older and budget Android phones as long as the dimensions match, and research will continue with goals including rough terrain locomotion, adding robotic arms, and running on-device LLMs. [Ahn Kwang-seop's AI Synthese] 280,000 Stars: Unmasking 'REA', the Project Shaking Up GitHub Covers the emergence and impact of 'REA', an open-source tool that drastically reduces the labor cost of reverse engineering by combining AI coding agents with specialized analysis tools. The GitHub project 'REA (Reverse Engineer Anything)' acts as an intermediate layer connecting specialized analysis tools (such as Ghidra and IDA) with AI coding agents, attracting immense attention in a short period. REA analyzes executable files, mobile apps, and websites to reconstruct operating rules, enabling agents to directly rewrite code and execute tests. With automated reverse engineering, the limitations of 'security through obscurity'—protecting logic by hiding it—have become clear, and it is expected to significantly impact areas that have relied on client-side security programs. [AI Now] Yesterday's Foes Become Allies... AI Sparks Realignment of Big Tech Frontlines As competition in AI technology intensifies, global Big Tech companies such as Apple, Meta, Google, and Microsoft are accelerating the realignment of their alliances—moving beyond past rivalries to collaborate, while simultaneously competing and clashing with existing partners. Apple and Meta, who had clashed for a long time over privacy issues, joined forces to optimize Meta's 'Muse Spark Open' model to run on Apple's 'Mac Studio'. Former partners Apple and OpenAI are engaged in legal disputes over allegations of mutual trade secret leaks during the development of new AI devices. Apple partnered with rival Google to co-develop the voice assistant Siri AI, while Microsoft is also strengthening its independent strategy by developing its own large-scale AI models and adopting Anthropic's models, even as it maintains cooperation with OpenAI."